><given-names>AC</given-names></name><name><surname>Stano</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><source>The Economics of Health and Health Care</source><year>2004</year><publisher-name>Upper Saddle River, NJ , Pearson Prentice Hall</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B7"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Santos Silva</surname><given-names>JMC</given-names></name><name><surname>Windmeijer</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Two-part multiple spell models for health care demand</article-title><source>Journal of Econometrics</source><year>2001</year><volume>104</volume><fpage>67</fpage><lpage>89</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0304-4076(01)00059-8</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B8"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pohlmeier</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Ulrich</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>An Econometric Model of the Two-Part Decisionmaking Process in the Demand for Health Care</article-title><source>The Journal of Human Resources</source><year>1995</year><volume>30</volume><fpage>339</fpage><lpage>361</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.2307/146123</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Newhouse</surname><given-names>JP</given-names></name></person-group><source>Free for all? Lessons from the RAND health insurance experiment</source><year>1993</year><publisher-name>Cambride, MA , Harvard University Press</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cameron</surname><given-names>AC</given-names></name><name><surname>Trivedi</surname><given-names>PK</given-names></name><name><surname>Milne</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><na